\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Axis units must be compatible.} In any two-parameter product system with multiplication maps $m_{s,t}$, a unit $u_{(a,b)}$ restricts to units $v_a=u_{(a,0)}$ and $w_b=u_{(0,b)}$. The two decompositions of $(a,b)$ force
\[
 m_{(a,0),(0,b)}(v_a\otimes w_b)
 =m_{(0,b),(a,0)}(w_b\otimes v_a).
\]
Thus independently choosing one-parameter units is insufficient; they must agree under the system's interchange map. This follows just by applying the unit law in the two orders, so has no hidden regularity assumption beyond that law.

\paragraph{An explicit discrete obstruction.} On the discrete semigroup $\mathbb N^2$, take one-dimensional fibers and multiplication $m_{s,t}(z\otimes w)=e^{i\theta s_2t_1}zw$. Associativity follows since $s_2t_1+(s_2+t_2)r_1=t_2r_1+s_2(t_1+r_1)$. A normalized unit would have nonzero axis values. Applying the preceding condition at $(1,0),(0,1)$ gives $vw=e^{i\theta}wv$, impossible unless $e^{i\theta}=1$. Each coordinate axis separately has plenty of units.

\paragraph{Source scope.} This scalar twisted example is discrete and is not asserted to be the source's reference-unit free construction, which already provides a compatible reference unit. It shows why nontrivial axis units need not extend. Determining the actual interchange relations for those free fibers, classifying all multiparameter units and deciding strong-commuting factorization of the induced Markov semigroups remain the first substantive gaps after construction.

